WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.690
In the last video, you saw how to define the content cost function for neural style transfer.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:09.370
Next, let's take a look at the style cost function.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.690
So what does the style of an image mean?

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.810
Let's say you have an input image like this, you're used to seeing a confident like that

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.450
compute features at various different hidden layers.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:26.770
And let's say you've chosen some layer L, maybe that layer, to define the measure of

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.170
the style of an image.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:35.330
What we're going to do is define the style as the correlation between activations across

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:40.770
different channels in this layer L activation.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.570
So here's what I mean by that.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.690
Let's say you take that layer L activation.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:51.130
So this is going to be a nh by nw by nc block of activations.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.270
And we're going to ask how correlated are the activations across different channels.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:02.750
So to explain what I mean by this maybe slightly cryptic phrase, let's take this block of activations

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.830
and let me shade the different channels that are different colors.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:13.190
So in this little example, we have say five channels, which is why I have five shades

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.190
of color here.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.950
In practice, of course, in neural network, we usually have a lot more channels than

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.750
five, but using just five makes the drawing easier.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.270
But to capture the style of an image, what you're going to do is the following.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.910
Let's look at the first two channels.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:35.670
Let's look at the red channel and the yellow channel and say how correlated are activations

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.590
in these first two channels.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:43.070
So for example, in the lower right hand corner, you have some activation in the first channel

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.990
and some activation in the second channel.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.910
So that gives you a pair of numbers.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:53.310
And what you do is look at different positions across this block of activations and just look

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:58.110
at those two pairs of numbers, one in the first channel, the red channel, one in the

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.190
yellow channel, the second channel.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:04.070
And you just look at these two pairs of numbers and see when you look across all of these

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:10.370
positions, all of these NH by NW positions, how correlated are these two numbers.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.710
So why does this capture style?

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.790
Let's look at an example.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.270
Here's one of the visualizations from the earlier video.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:23.670
This comes from again the paper by Matthew Zieler and Rob Fergus that I referenced earlier.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.870
And let's say for the sake of argument that the red neuron corresponds to, and let's say

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:34.630
for the sake of argument that the red channel corresponds to this neuron.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:40.950
So it's trying to figure out if there's this little vertical texture in a particular

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.190
position in the image.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:52.150
And let's say that this second channel, this yellow second channel corresponds to this neuron,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.750
which is vaguely looking for orange colored patches.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:00.110
So what does it mean for these two channels to be highly correlated?

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:05.390
Well, if they're highly correlated, what that means is whenever part of the image

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:10.550
has this type of subtle vertical texture, that part of the image will probably have

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:13.150
this orange-ish tint.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.390
And what does it mean for them to be uncorrelated?

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:21.150
Well, it means that whenever there is this vertical texture, it probably won't have that

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.830
orange-ish tint.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:28.590
And so the correlation tells you which of these high-level texture components tend to

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.870
occur or not occur together in part of an image.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:38.150
And it's the degree of correlation that gives you one way of measuring how often these

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:44.030
different high-level features, such as vertical texture or this orange tint or other

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:49.270
things as well, how often they occur and how often they occur together and don't occur

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.870
together in different parts of an image.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:57.470
And so if we use the degree of correlation between channels as a measure of the style,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:03.430
then what you can do is measure the degree to which in your generated image, this first

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:08.430
channel is correlated or uncorrelated with the second channel.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:14.310
And that will tell you in the generated image how often this type of vertical texture occurs

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.270
or doesn't occur with this orange-ish tint.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:22.110
And this gives you a measure of how similar is the style of the generated image to the

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.910
style of the input style image.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.870
So let's now formalize this intuition.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:34.870
So what you're going to do is, given an image, compute something called a style matrix, which

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:39.190
will measure all those correlations we talked about on the last slide.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:46.470
So more formally, let's let A superscript L subscript i, j, k denote the activation at

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:54.390
position i, j, k in hidden layer L. So i indexes into the height, j indexes into the

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:58.230
width, and k indexes across the different channels.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:05.450
So in the previous slide, we had five channels, but k would index across those five channels.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.990
So what the style matrix will do is you're going to compute a matrix called this G superscript

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:17.410
square bracket L. This is going to be an NC by NC dimensional matrix.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.270
So it'll be a square matrix.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:25.710
Remember you have NC channels, and so you have an NC by NC dimensional matrix in order

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.750
to measure how correlated each pair of them is.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:37.390
So in particular, GL, k, k prime will measure how correlated are the activations in channel

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:44.430
k compared to the activations in channel k prime, where here k and k prime will range

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:51.900
from 1 through NC, the number of channels there are in that layer.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:58.780
So more formally, the way you compute GL, and I'm just going to write down the formula

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.860
for computing one element.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:09.660
So the k, k prime elements of this, this is going to be sum over i, sum over j of the

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:23.150
activation in that layer i, j, k times the activation at i, j, k prime.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:28.150
So here, remember i and j index across the different positions in the block.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.430
Index is over the height and width.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:43.630
So i is the sum from 1 to nh, and j is the sum from 1 to nw, and k here and k prime

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.070
index over the channel.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:51.470
So k and k prime range from 1 to the total number of channels in that layer of the neural

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.680
network.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.680
So what this is doing is summing over the different positions of the image over the

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:05.240
height and width, and just multiplying the activations together of the channels k and

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:09.120
k prime, and that's the definition of G, k, k prime.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:15.280
And you do this for every value of k and k prime to compute this matrix G, also called

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.750
the style matrix.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:25.110
And so notice that if both of these activations tend to be lodged together, then G, k, k

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:29.510
prime will be lodged, whereas if they are uncorrelated, then G, k, k prime might be

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:30.750
small.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:35.510
And technically, I've been using the term correlation to convey intuition, but this

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:40.830
is actually the unnormalized cross covariance because we're not subtracting out the

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:46.290
mean and this is just multiplying out these elements directly.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:50.910
So this is how you compute the style of an image.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:55.990
And you'd actually do this for both the style image S and for the generated image G.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:05.520
So just to distinguish that this is the style image, maybe let me add a round bracket S

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:11.320
there, just to denote that this is the style image for the image S, and those are the

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.750
activations on the image S.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:21.190
And what you do is then compute the same thing for the generated image.

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:33.310
So it's really the same thing, sum of i's, sum of j, aijkl, aijkl, and the summation

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:41.600
indices are the same as follows.

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:47.560
And you want to just denote this is the generated image, I'll just put the round

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.140
brackets G there.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.860
So now you have two matrices that capture what is the style of the image S and what

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:59.740
is the style of the image G.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:05.700
And by the way, we've been using the alphabet capital G to denote these matrices.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:11.740
In linear algebra, these are also called the grand matrix, or these are called grand

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:12.740
matrices.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:17.300
But in this video, I'm just going to use the term style matrix, but this is term

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:23.260
grand matrix that motivates using capital G to denote these matrices.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:31.020
Finally, the cost function, the style cost function, if you're doing this on layer L,

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:46.770
between S and G, you can now define that to be just the difference between these two

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:53.250
matrices, GLG squared.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.250
And these are matrices.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.330
So I'll just take the Frobenius norm.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:10:01.490
So this is just the sum of squares of the element-wise differences between these two

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:02.490
matrices.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:09.810
And just to write this out, this is going to be sum over K, sum over K prime of

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:10.810
these differences.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:26.760
S, K, K prime minus GLG, K, K prime, and then the sum of squares of elements.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:34.120
The authors actually use this for the normalization constants, 2 times the nH, nW,

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.720
that layer, and C, that layer, and then square this.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.320
You can put this up here as well.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:44.160
But the normalization constant doesn't matter that much because this cost is

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.480
multiplied by some hyperparameter B anyway.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:53.320
So just to finish up, this is the style cost function defined using layer L.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:59.000
And as you saw on the previous slide, this is basically the Frobenius norm

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:06.600
between the two style matrices computed on the image S and on the image G,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.800
Frobenius norm squared, and then with additional normalization constants,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.640
which isn't that important.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:19.200
And finally, it turns out that you get more visually pleasing results if you use

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.600
the style cost function from multiple different layers.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:32.040
So the overall style cost function you can define as sum over all the different

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:39.360
layers of the style cost function for that layer, which we've defined above,

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:44.200
weighted by some set of parameters, by some set of additional hyperparameters,

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.120
which you want to know as lambda L here.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:51.640
So what this does is it allows you to use different layers in the neural network,

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:55.840
both the earlier ones, which measure relatively simpler low-level features

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:01.200
like edges, as well as some later layers, which measure high-level features

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.960
and cause the neural network to take both low-level and high-level

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.720
correlations into account when computing style.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:14.000
And in the program exercise, you gain more intuition about what might be

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.120
the reasonable choices for this hyperparameter lambda as well.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:25.120
And so just to wrap this up, you can now define the overall cost function

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:33.960
as alpha times the content cost between C and G plus beta times the style cost

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:38.960
between S and G, and then use gradient descent or a more sophisticated

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:43.520
optimization algorithm if you want in order to try to find an image G

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:48.640
that tries to minimize this cost function, J of G.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:54.240
And if you do that, you can generate pretty good-looking neural artistic.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:59.240
And if you do that, you'll be able to generate some pretty nice novel artwork.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:03.360
So that's it for neural style transfer, and I hope you have fun implementing it

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.440
in this week's program exercise.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.600
Before wrapping up this week, there's just one last thing on the share view,

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:15.840
which is how to do convolutions over 1D or 3D data rather than over only 2D images.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.080
Let's go on to the last video.
